Attribution
-----------

Suggested form:

    Benchmark data from the jayn project (https://github.com/jaynapp/jayn),
    Copyright 2026 Hugo Puybareau, Maxence Rossignol and the jayn
    contributors, licensed under CC BY 4.0.

Cite the specific fitted file (for example `curves/caveman-style.json`) when quoting
a particular figure. The numbers are per-command measurements against one build
of one tool, not a single headline rate, and a fitted file carries the batches it was
fitted from so a citation can be traced back to the observations behind it.

Who holds the copyright
-----------------------

Contributing a batch assigns nothing. Whoever measured it keeps whatever rights
exist in it and licenses it here under CC BY 4.0, on the same terms as the
maintainers. The collective line above is shorthand, not a claim over anyone
else's measurements, and it is why that line never has to be edited when someone
contributes.

Each batch names its author in its own sidecar, under `contributor`:

    data/caveman/<batch>.meta.json

That handle is the designation this licence asks you to credit, and it is
enforced rather than assumed — a sidecar naming nobody is rejected on read
instead of being fitted, because the failure mode is silent
otherwise: the measurement would end up credited to the maintainers. Every
fitted row lists the batches behind it, so a figure traces to its authors
without a separate index to maintain.

A note on whether any of this is copyrightable
----------------------------------------------

Possibly not, and the licence is here anyway. An observation is two token counts
measured from a fixture this repository generated — a fact, and facts attract no
copyright in the United States and several other jurisdictions. CC BY 4.0 is
used because it also licenses the sui generis database right where one does
exist, notably in the EU, and because stating the terms plainly is worth more
than being right about which of them bite.
